/-
# Local sphericity from the acyclicity of the closed cells

For a `PolyCW` `P`, an acyclicity datum `IsCellAcyclic P hdd` (the cellular chain complex of the
closed cell of every cell has the homology of a point) implies that the subdivision is locally
spherical: the local complex

`locCx P e = leSub e ⧸ ltSub e`

of every cell `e` has the homology of a sphere, generated by the class of the subdivision chain
`subdivCell e`.  Equivalently, `locMap P hdd haug e` is a quasi-isomorphism for every cell `e`.

## Strategy

Everything is done with the *support-based* (localized, "star") filtrations of `SupportCx.lean`:
for a lower finset `S` of cells, `cellS P hdd S` and `sdS P S` are the subcomplexes supported on
`S`, and `stageMap P hdd haug S : cellS S ⟶ sdS S` is the restriction of the subdivision.  Three
statements are combined.

* **(A) the boundary.**  `boundaryStage_quasiIso`: for `S = ioF e` (the proper faces of `e`), the
  subdivision `stageMap P hdd haug (ioF e)` is a quasi-isomorphism as soon as the local condition
  holds at every cell of `ioF e`.  This is the cell-by-cell induction `stageLevel_quasiIso` of
  `StageInduction.lean` applied to the lower set `ioF e`, whose cells all have dimension `< dim e`.

* **(B) the closed cell.**  `stageMap_icF_quasiIso`: for `S = icF e` (the closed cell of `e`), the
  subdivision `stageMap P hdd haug (icF e)` is a quasi-isomorphism.  Both sides are acyclic with
  `H₀ ≅ ℤ` — the cellular side by `IsCellAcyclic`, the subdivided side by the explicit cone
  contraction `leSubRetr` of `leSubRetr_quasiIso.lean` — and the induced map on `H₀` is surjective
  because a `0`-cell of `e` maps to the subdividing vertex.  The abstract criterion is
  `quasiIso_of_comp_unitCx`: a chain map `K ⟶ unitCx 0` with `H₀(K) ≅ ℤ`, `H_i(K)` torsion-free
  (here zero) for `i ≠ 0` and surjective in degree `0` is a quasi-isomorphism; it is applied to
  the composite `stageMap (icF e) ≫ leSubRetr e`, and `QuasiIso` descends to `stageMap (icF e)`
  because `leSubRetr` is itself a quasi-isomorphism.

* **(C) the local map.**  `locMap_quasiIso_of_stage` (imported): for a stage `T` of a cell `a`
  (`IsStage`), the local map `locMap a` is a quasi-isomorphism as soon as `stageMap` is one on `T`
  and on `insert a T`.  It is the third-term five lemma `quasiIso_quotMap_of_sub` applied to the
  pair of pieces `cellPieceS P hdd a h ⊆ cellS P hdd (insert a T)` and
  `sdPieceS P a h ⊆ sdS P (insert a T)`, together with the identification
  `stageQuotMap_eq_locMap` of the resulting quotient map with `locMap a`.

For a cell `e` with `T = ioF e` (a stage, `isStage_ioF`), (C) needs (A) at `ioF e` and (B) at
`icF e`, and `insert e (ioF e) = icF e` (`closedCell_insert_ioF`).  (A) at `ioF e` involves the
local conditions only at cells of dimension `< dim e`, so the induction on the dimension closes
(`locMap_quasiIso`), and the theorem follows.
-/
import Mathlib
